window.docContents[128].resolve({"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___16___-Problems-on-Lie-algebras/Quantum-enveloping-algebra-U_q_LPAR_sl_LPAR_2_RPAR__RPAR_-and-its-irreducible-representations/#Chapter2___Problem2___16___5___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.16. Problems on Lie algebras\u0009Quantum enveloping algebra U_q(sl(2)) and its irreducible representations\u0009Quantum enveloping algebra U_q(sl(2)) and its irreducible representations","header":"Formalization","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___16___-Problems-on-Lie-algebras/Quantum-enveloping-algebra-U_q_LPAR_sl_LPAR_2_RPAR__RPAR_-and-its-irreducible-representations/#Chapter2___Problem2___16___5___formalization"},"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___3___-Representations/Corollary-2___3___10-is-false-over-the-reals/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___3___-Representations--Corollary-2___3___10-is-false-over-the-reals--Corollary-2___3___10-is-false-over-the-reals--Formalization--Primary-declarations":{"contents":"Multiplication by the imaginary unit on the complex numbers is not multiplication by a real scalar.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Remark2.3.11; role=primary]\n\nThe proposed assertion that every such endomorphism is scalar multiplication is false.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Remark2.3.11; role=primary]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.3. Representations\u0009Corollary 2.3.10 is false over the reals\u0009Corollary 2.3.10 is false over the reals\u0009Formalization","header":"Primary declarations","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___3___-Representations/Corollary-2___3___10-is-false-over-the-reals/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___3___-Representations--Corollary-2___3___10-is-false-over-the-reals--Corollary-2___3___10-is-false-over-the-reals--Formalization--Primary-declarations"},"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___8___-Quivers/Generators-and-relations-for-the-path-algebra/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___8___-Quivers--Generators-and-relations-for-the-path-algebra--Generators-and-relations-for-the-path-algebra--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"Compatible orthogonal vertex idempotents and oppositely oriented arrow elements determine a unique algebra homomorphism from the opposite path algebra.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Problem2.8.6; role=supporting]\n\nAn opposite arrow element multiplied on the right by its source vertex element is unchanged.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Problem2.8.6; role=supporting]\n\nAn opposite arrow element multiplied on the right by a different vertex element is zero.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Problem2.8.6; role=supporting]\n\nDistinct opposite vertex elements multiply to zero.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Problem2.8.6; role=supporting]\n\nEvery opposite vertex element is idempotent.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Problem2.8.6; role=supporting]\n\nFor a finite quiver, the sum of all opposite vertex elements is one.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Problem2.8.6; role=supporting]\n\nThe target vertex element multiplied on the left by an opposite arrow element leaves the arrow unchanged.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Problem2.8.6; role=supporting]\n\nA vertex element different from the target multiplied on the left by an opposite arrow element is zero.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Problem2.8.6; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.8. Quivers\u0009Generators and relations for the path algebra\u0009Generators and relations for the path algebra\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___8___-Quivers/Generators-and-relations-for-the-path-algebra/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___8___-Quivers--Generators-and-relations-for-the-path-algebra--Generators-and-relations-for-the-path-algebra--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"},"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___1___-Subrepresentations-in-semisimple-representations/Classification-of-subrepresentations-in-semisimple-representations/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--3___-General-results-of-representation-theory--3___1___-Subrepresentations-in-semisimple-representations--Classification-of-subrepresentations-in-semisimple-representations--Classification-of-subrepresentations-in-semisimple-representations--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"Declaration: IsSemisimpleModule.eq_bot_or_exists_simple_le\n\nAlignment metadata: book-ref=Chapter3/Proposition3.1.4/Derived4; role=supporting\n\nA submodule of a displayed finite function module is equivalent to one with bounded multiplicities.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Proposition3.1.4; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Theorem3.2.2/Derived4; role=supporting]\n\nA submodule of the displayed direct sum admits bounded multiplicities and linearly independent\ncoordinate data satisfying the stated expansion formula.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Discussion_after_Lemma3.1.6/Derived4; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Proposition3.1.4; role=supporting]\n\nA submodule of the displayed function module admits bounded multiplicities and linearly\nindependent coordinate data satisfying the stated expansion formula.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Corollary3.2.1/Derived2; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Proposition3.1.4; role=supporting]\n\nA finite function module with values in a simple module is isotypic of that type.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Proposition3.1.4/Derived4; role=supporting]\n\nA submodule of the displayed direct sum admits bounded multiplicities and injective coordinate data.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Discussion_after_Lemma3.1.6/Derived4; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Proposition3.1.4; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Remark3.1.5; role=primary]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00093. General results of representation theory\u00093.1. Subrepresentations in semisimple representations\u0009Classification of subrepresentations in semisimple representations\u0009Classification of subrepresentations in semisimple representations\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___1___-Subrepresentations-in-semisimple-representations/Classification-of-subrepresentations-in-semisimple-representations/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--3___-General-results-of-representation-theory--3___1___-Subrepresentations-in-semisimple-representations--Classification-of-subrepresentations-in-semisimple-representations--Classification-of-subrepresentations-in-semisimple-representations--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___13___-Proof-of-the-classification-theorem-for-representations-of-___S_n___/a_lambda-x-b_lambda-___-l_lambda_LPAR_x_RPAR_-c_lambda-where-l_lambda-is-a-linear-function/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___13___-Proof-of-the-classification-theorem-for-representations-of-___S_n___--a_lambda-x-b_lambda-___-l_lambda_LPAR_x_RPAR_-c_lambda-where-l_lambda-is-a-linear-function--a_lambda-x-b_lambda-___-l_lambda_LPAR_x_RPAR_-c_lambda-where-l_lambda-is-a-linear-function--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"For each partition, there is a function-like scalar-valued object such that placing any group-algebra element between the two displayed elements gives its scalar value times their product.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Lemma5.13.1; role=supporting]\n\nIf a permutation does not lie in the product of the two displayed sets, there is a swap in the first set whose conjugate by that permutation lies in the second set.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Discussion_end_of_Lemma5.13.1_proof; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Lemma5.13.1; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.13. Proof of the classification theorem for representations of $S_n$\u0009a_lambda x b_lambda = l_lambda(x) c_lambda where l_lambda is a linear function\u0009a_lambda x b_lambda = l_lambda(x) c_lambda where l_lambda is a linear function\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___13___-Proof-of-the-classification-theorem-for-representations-of-___S_n___/a_lambda-x-b_lambda-___-l_lambda_LPAR_x_RPAR_-c_lambda-where-l_lambda-is-a-linear-function/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___13___-Proof-of-the-classification-theorem-for-representations-of-___S_n___--a_lambda-x-b_lambda-___-l_lambda_LPAR_x_RPAR_-c_lambda-where-l_lambda-is-a-linear-function--a_lambda-x-b_lambda-___-l_lambda_LPAR_x_RPAR_-c_lambda-where-l_lambda-is-a-linear-function--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___9___-The-Frobenius-formula-for-the-character-of-an-induced-representation/Alternative-form-of-the-Frobenius-formula/#Chapter5___Remark5___9___2":{"contents":"Remark 5.9.2. If the characteristic of the ground field k is relatively prime to |H|, then this formula can be written as\n\n\\chi(g) = \\frac{1}{|H|} \\sum_{x \\in G : xgx^{-1} \\in H} \\chi_V(xgx^{-1}).\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.9. The Frobenius formula for the character of an induced representation\u0009Alternative form of the Frobenius formula","header":"Alternative form of the Frobenius formula","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___9___-The-Frobenius-formula-for-the-character-of-an-induced-representation/Alternative-form-of-the-Frobenius-formula/#Chapter5___Remark5___9___2"},"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___5___-Blocks/Linked-simple-modules-and-blocks/#Chapter9___Definition9___5___1":{"contents":"Definition 9.5.1. Two simple finite dimensional A-modules X, Y are said to be linked if there is a chain X = M_0, M_1, \\ldots, M_n = Y such that for each i = 0, \\ldots, n - 1 either \\operatorname{Ext}^1(M_i, M_{i+1}) \\neq 0 or \\operatorname{Ext}^1(M_{i+1}, M_i) \\neq 0 (or both).\nHere we agree that X is linked to itself (by a chain of length 0).\n\nThis linking relation is clearly an equivalence relation, so it defines a splitting of the set S of isomorphism classes of simple A-modules into equivalence classes S_k, k \\in B. The kth block \\mathcal{C}_k of \\mathcal{C} is, by definition, the category of all objects M of \\mathcal{C} such that all simple modules occurring in the Jordan-Hölder series of M are in S_k.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00099. Structure of finite dimensional algebras\u00099.5. Blocks\u0009Linked simple modules and blocks","header":"Linked simple modules and blocks","id":"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___5___-Blocks/Linked-simple-modules-and-blocks/#Chapter9___Definition9___5___1"},"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___5___-Blocks/Section-9___5___-Blocks/#Chapter9___Introduction_9___5":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00099. Structure of finite dimensional algebras\u00099.5. Blocks\u0009Section 9.5: Blocks","header":"Section 9.5: Blocks","id":"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___5___-Blocks/Section-9___5___-Blocks/#Chapter9___Introduction_9___5"},"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___6___-Finite-abelian-categories/Finite-abelian-category/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras--9___6___-Finite-abelian-categories--Finite-abelian-category--Finite-abelian-category--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"An auxiliary type associated to the category.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter9/Definition9.6.1; role=supporting]\n\nThe subobjects of any object form a finite-dimensional order.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter9/Definition9.6.1; role=supporting]\n\nEach object's subobject order is finite-dimensional.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter9/Definition9.6.1; role=supporting]\n\nThe opaque auxiliary type is finite.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter9/Definition9.6.1; role=supporting]\n\nThe object of the category associated to an auxiliary index.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter9/Definition9.6.1; role=supporting]\n\nEvery simple object is isomorphic to an object indexed by the auxiliary type.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter9/Definition9.6.1; role=supporting]\n\nThe object associated to an auxiliary index is simple.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter9/Definition9.6.1; role=supporting]\n\n* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter9/Definition9.6.1; role=supporting]\n\n* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter9/Definition9.6.1; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00099. Structure of finite dimensional algebras\u00099.6. Finite abelian categories\u0009Finite abelian category\u0009Finite abelian category\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___6___-Finite-abelian-categories/Finite-abelian-category/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras--9___6___-Finite-abelian-categories--Finite-abelian-category--Finite-abelian-category--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"}});